course: dictionary
lesson: 13881
title: Words from history and honours
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Knighthood is a noun. It can mean the honour of being a knight, or the rank of a knight in the Middle Ages. Clues around the word tell you which meaning fits.
explain: «She was proud of her knighthood, given for years of helping sick children.» The words proud and given for helping point to an honour. Try swapping in «the honour of being a knight» and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «In the Middle Ages, his knighthood meant he served a lord on horseback.» The clue Middle Ages points to history, so here knighthood means the rank of a trained soldier. Always check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Knighthood is a noun: the title and honour of being a knight.
fact: In the UK, the king or queen gives a knighthood to people for great service, and a man is then called «Sir».
fact: A woman given the same honour is given a damehood and is called «Dame».
fact: In the Middle Ages, knighthood was the rank of a trained soldier on horseback who served a lord.
fact: Today, famous people such as Sir David Attenborough have been given knighthoods for their work in science and nature.
exercise: «The queen gave him a knighthood for his years of service to charity.» What does knighthood mean here? | A castle on a hill | A group of people who sing | A sword made of steel | *The honour of being made a knight
exercise: «In the Middle Ages, his knighthood meant he served a lord on horseback.» What is knighthood in this sentence? | A type of warm coat | A kind of cake | *The rank of a trained soldier on horseback | A school for young children
exercise: A woman given the same honour as a knight is called what? | Duke | Knight | Sir | *Dame
test: Which title is used for a man who has been given a knighthood? | Master | *Sir | Lord | Dame
test: In the Middle Ages, what was a knight? | *A trained soldier on horseback who served a lord | A farmer who grew wheat | A baker who made bread | A doctor who visited the sick
exam: Which sentence uses knighthood correctly? | *Her years of service to charity earned her a knighthood | The knighthood swam across the river | He drank a knighthood of warm tea | The castle had a fine knighthood on its walls
